course: adult-life
lesson: 31
title: Votes, parties and the people in charge
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In a democracy the grown-ups choose who runs the country and the town. They do it by voting in an election, and the people they choose make the laws and spend the taxes.
explain: Before an election, leaflets with smiling faces land on the mat: each candidate explains what they promise. On the day, the school hall becomes a polling station, and each voter puts a cross on a paper in a little booth, in secret.
explain: Scene: at Christmas dinner in Leeds, Grandad and Auntie Jo argue about the government until the gravy goes cold. Later Grandad winks at you: «Who I voted for is between me and the ballot box.» Nobody was wrong to argue, and nobody has to say how they voted.
explain: People belong to parties, each with its own colour and its own ideas about taxes, schools, hospitals and who may come to live in the country. They disagree honestly, and the next election lets everyone choose again.
fact: An election is when adults vote to choose their leaders and representatives; candidates send leaflets to explain their promises.
fact: A polling station is a place, often a school or church hall, where people vote in secret in a small booth.
fact: Voting is secret, so nobody has to tell anyone how they voted, not even their own family.
fact: A political party is a group of people who share ideas about how the country should be run; colours like red, blue, yellow and green help people recognise them.
fact: Family arguments about politics are very common, because big choices affect everybody and people's views come from their own lives.
fact: In the UK the Prime Minister leads the government from 10 Downing Street; in the USA the leader is the President, and both must obey the law.
fact: Many people feel let down by politicians, but they can still vote, write letters and get involved.
fact: Parliament is where elected representatives, called MPs in the UK, debate and vote on new laws.
fact: Tax is money people pay to the government from what they earn and spend, and it pays for schools, hospitals, roads and police.
fact: The NHS is the UK's National Health Service: medical care free when you need it, paid for by taxes.
fact: Immigration means people moving to live in another country; countries have rules about it, and people disagree about those rules.
fact: In the UK grown-ups can vote from the age of 18; children cannot vote yet, but they can still write letters and have their say.
exercise: Leaflets come through the door saying «Vote for me on Thursday!» What is going on? | There is an election and candidates want votes | The council is selling off some of its houses on Thursday | A new shop is opening on Thursday | The school is choosing a new head teacher
exercise: Mum says «the school hall is a polling station today». What happens there? | People go in to vote in secret | Children sit their exams there | People queue for free school dinners | The police check everyone's papers
exercise: Grandad says «That's between me and the ballot box.» What does he mean? | His vote is secret and he'd rather not say | He forgot who he voted for | He is not allowed to vote any more because he is too old | He put his vote in the wrong box
exercise: Why do people on TV wear red, blue, yellow or green rosettes at election time? | To show which political party they belong to | To show which football team they support | To show they have won a prize | To show they work at the polling station
exercise: Grandad and Auntie argue about the government at Christmas. What is the best way to see it? | They have different views, and that is normal | One of them must be a bad person for voting that way | Families are not allowed to talk politics | The government told them to argue
exercise: Who usually speaks outside the famous black door of 10 Downing Street? | The Prime Minister, who leads the UK government | The King, who makes all the laws in the country himself | The Mayor of London, who runs the country | The head of the BBC, who chooses leaders
exercise: Uncle mutters «They're all as bad as each other.» What does he feel? | Let down by politicians in general | Proud of every politician | Happy with the government | Sure that one party is perfect
exercise: The news says «MPs voted on the new law in Parliament.» What happened? | Elected representatives voted on a new law | Children voted on new school rules | The police chose a brand new law just for themselves | The Prime Minister wrote a law alone
exercise: Dad looks at his payslip: «Look how much the taxman takes!» What is the money used for? | Schools, hospitals, roads and police | Only the Prime Minister's big house in London | A savings account for Dad later | Prizes for people who pay first
exercise: Mum says «Thank goodness for the NHS, even if it was five hours.» What is the NHS? | The UK's health service, free when you need it | A private hospital you must pay for every time you visit | A charity that only helps children | A shop that sells medicine cheaply
exercise: Grown-ups on the news argue about people moving to the country. What is this called? | Immigration | Election | Taxation | Recycling
exercise: Is it OK to ask Grandad who he voted for? | Yes, and it is fine if he doesn't answer | No, asking is against the law | Yes, and he must always tell you | No, children must never ask about voting
exercise: Why is voting done in secret? | So nobody can pressure or punish you for your choice | So the counting can be done faster on the night | So nobody knows an election is happening | So children can sneak in and vote without being seen
exercise: At what age can people vote in most UK elections? | 18 | 12 | 21 | 30
exercise: Can people criticise the Prime Minister in a free country? | Yes, criticising leaders is allowed | No, it is a crime to criticise leaders | Only other politicians may criticise him | Only on the day of the election
exercise: Leaflets promise lots of things. What can you do with them? | Read them and ask what the promises mean | Believe every promise without asking | Throw them away because they are always lies | Post them back to make the person lose
exercise: Who are the families being discussed in the immigration news? | Real people who deserve respect | Characters made up for the news | Only people who broke the law | Nobody, it is just about numbers
test: Your brother says «The Prime Minister makes all the laws on his own.» Put him right. | MPs debate and vote on laws in Parliament | Yes, the Prime Minister writes every law alone | The King makes laws and MPs just watch | The police make laws and judges sign them
test: Overheard on the phone: «Popped into the church hall on the way to work, put my cross in, job done.» What did she do? | She voted at a polling station | She went to a church service | She signed up to clean the hall | She posted a parcel for work
test: Uncle at the telly: «Bloody politicians, all promises and no delivery.» What is he grumbling about? | He thinks politicians break their promises | He thinks the post is always late | He is angry about a parcel he ordered that never came | He wants politicians to deliver leaflets
test: Gossip at the school gate: «She's voting for the greens, can you believe it? Lovely woman otherwise.» What is the kind way to think about this? | People can pick different parties and still be good | Picking another party makes her a bad person | Mums are not allowed to talk about voting | Green is the only colour anyone should vote for
test: If there were no taxes, what would happen to free schools and ambulances? | They would not exist, because taxes pay for them | They would carry on exactly the same as they do now | They would be paid for by the Prime Minister | They would be free because hospitals are rich
test: Your cousin in Leeds says «Grandad HAS to tell us who he voted for, he's family.» Is that right? | No, a vote is secret, even from family | Yes, family must always share their votes | Yes, but only at Christmas dinner | No, because Grandad isn't allowed to vote
test: Your friend says «NHS means you pay the doctor yourself, innit.» Put him right. | No, NHS care is free when you need it, paid by taxes | Yes, you pay the doctor every time you go | Yes, but only children have to pay | No, NHS doctors are all volunteers who never earn anything
test: A girl in your class moved from Poland last year. The news is arguing about immigration. Which is true? | The news is about real families like hers | The news has nothing to do with real people | She should be kept away from the class | Immigration means people moving to new houses
test: In the USA, who has the job that the Prime Minister has in the UK? | The President | The Queen | The Mayor | The Sheriff
test: Mia, aged 10, wants the park swings fixed and the law changed. What can she do? | Write a letter to her local MP | Wait until she is 18 to say anything | Go and vote at the polling station | Nothing, children have no say at all
test: Dad mutters «Taxman takes a third, I swear.» Mum: «And who drives on the roads, eh?» What is Mum pointing out? | Taxes pay for things Dad uses, like roads | The taxman drives on Dad's roads for free | Dad doesn't pay any tax at all | Roads are paid for by the car makers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle of the secret ballot? | No elector is obliged to disclose how he or she has voted | Every elector must declare the vote to the family | The vote of each elector is published the following day | Only children are permitted to know how votes are cast
exam: Identify the institution in which elected representatives debate and vote upon proposed legislation in the United Kingdom. | Parliament | The polling station | The council tip | The National Health Service
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning taxation. | It is levied upon earnings and spending and finances public services | It is a voluntary gift that only wealthy persons provide to the nation | It is retained by the Prime Minister for personal use | It is collected solely to finance political parties
exam: It is given that relatives disagree vehemently regarding the government. Determine the most appropriate characterisation. | A common and legitimate difference of political opinion | Evidence that one relative has committed an offence | A matter which the law forbids families to discuss | Proof that one of the political parties is wicked
